:answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368148} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4643b74-0553-58af-a597-f560a8bf34f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4643b74-0553-58af-a597-f560a8bf34f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368151} :answer-field/id #uuid "24980e91-6228-5dad-a305-d83da3126fce" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52a5f397-6ab6-5b30-a51f-fa3f7be9877d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335130" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n| ${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+2y \\\\ -3x+{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3x+{y}^{2})=-3,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3x+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(0) & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221418 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9aeac73-8ee3-5fef-a47c-a96e3545d89c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Linear Approximations Near Equilibria"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed2047a8-8982-5263-b25f-46e01db0123d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed2047a8-8982-5263-b25f-46e01db0123d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368177} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a3a8240-1762-543d-99d2-3372e89367f7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368176} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2da43c9-f82c-5c88-b1e6-691df9d8c9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2da43c9-f82c-5c88-b1e6-691df9d8c9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368179} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d3bca15-6c24-52f3-baa6-76c81a9a965b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57371dc2-00f9-5847-bcf0-bf21a9eee108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57371dc2-00f9-5847-bcf0-bf21a9eee108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368181} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8f71a2-83e7-5924-ab0b-12a5484830f6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368180} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e2d6566-7c2c-5700-9daf-b1f1f46b1e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e2d6566-7c2c-5700-9daf-b1f1f46b1e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368183} :answer-field/id #uuid "3396f4c5-a888-5322-87d4-018f368e9efa" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "396d0927-5f69-505a-80d1-a119800f214e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336900" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-6 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-3y(t)+{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}?$\n\n| ${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+2y-6 \\\\ x-3y+{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2y-6)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2y-6)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-3y+{y}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-3y+{y}^{2})=-3+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2y-3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(2) & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2(1)-3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221419 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "777fae40-2377-5e63-89f6-5c857beba2b2" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying an Isolated Critical Point of a Nonlinear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54ea5697-e4ef-54af-b81f-7e25b01bce9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368203} {:answer/id #uuid "9810ecd9-fc7e-5f2f-a0da-32c49e519e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54ea5697-e4ef-54af-b81f-7e25b01bce9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368203} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee66c07-aedb-5dc2-9782-17c5870e5ecd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368202} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bc40fa7-8e1e-5f9b-ad4e-31114d9b1e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368206} {:answer/id #uuid "70f5be1c-7ce8-5c70-9672-5f4d7be10919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368207} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6e4ec8-2ac9-5ab9-907d-334bae85047c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70f5be1c-7ce8-5c70-9672-5f4d7be10919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368207} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a03ba21-b40c-5305-8798-d44f1ddced2f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ce4d3c5-f9b2-510f-a42c-6aedb52f013f" :question/math-academy-id "q-337695" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t)+x(t)^{3} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+y(t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y+{x}^{3} \\\\ -2x+{y}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =2+3{x}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2+3{x}^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2+0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=1\\pm i.$\n\nSince we have two complex eigenvalues with positive real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{spiral source}.$"}]}
